Suppose you're writing a to-do list. Each row has a title and an integer ID.
Two rows can have the same title, so a rename message needs both the ID of
the row to change and the new text. A choice describes the actions the list
understands. Each variant names one action and the fields it needs.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    TodoMessage = {
        Rename | id I32, text String |,
        Delete | id I32 |,
    }

    message = TodoMessage::Rename(42, "Overthrow government")
]

This works when you know both pieces of information. Now consider a text
editor for the row. When you set it up, you know which title to display and
which row it belongs to. The person editing the title hasn't typed the new
text yet.

If the editor reports only `"Overthrow government"`, the list still has to work out
which row changed. One solution keeps a table of editors and row IDs, then
looks up the ID for every edit. Another makes the editor remember the row ID
and build `TodoMessage::Rename` itself. That editor now knows about to-do
items. A shopping list with a different message type needs its own version.

Some languages let the caller give the editor a *closure*, a function that
can remember values from where you defined it. For this row, the function
could remember 42, take the new text as its argument and return a rename
message. The editor could call it without knowing anything about to-do items.

Moth lets the caller describe this particular job with two parameters. A `Wire` says which binding supplies
the title. A `Route` says which message constructor to use and which field to
fill when the new text arrives. Moth can check those choices at the call site,
before any UI code handles an edit.
